  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 AM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nFog has developed across parts of northern IN, mainly just east\nof the cwa border and other then some very shallow patchy fog\nand mainly across northwest IN early this morning, not expecting\nany widespread fog concerns.\n\nAn upper ridge will build across the region today bringing high\ntemps in the mid 80s north to upper 80s south and made no\nchanges to the previous forecast. Current cloud cover is\nexpected to thin by midday and if mostly sunny skies materialize,\nits possible high temps may be a degree or two warmer then\ncurrently forecast. As the low levels mix and low level winds\nincrease, southwest winds may gust into the 25-30 mph range this\nafternoon and these gusts may continue into this evening and\nperiodically overnight as strong low level winds are expected\ntonight. And while temps are expected to quickly drop through\nthe 70s early this evening, temps will level off in the upper \n60s to near 70 for lows by Tuesday morning.\n\nA weak trough of low pressure will move across the area Tuesday\nmorning, shifting winds westerly but with little change to the \nairmass, high temps on Tuesday are likely to be nearly the same\nas today, ranging from lower 80s across northern IL, mid 80s for\nmuch of the Chicago metro and upper 80s for areas south of \nI-80. With dewpoints creeping into the mid 60s on Tuesday, heat \nindex values south of I-80 may climb into the lower 90s Tuesday \nafternoon.\n\nPrecip chances for today and tonight, while already low, now\nappear too low for any mention and will have a dry forecast\nthrough Tuesday afternoon. If any isolated showers were to \ndevelop, the best time window appears to be around/just after \ndaybreak Tuesday morning.\n\nA cold front will move south into the area late Tuesday\nafternoon and slowly move south across the area Tuesday night.\nThis will be the time period when showers and scattered\nthunderstorms are possible. Precipitable water values range\nbetween 1.5 and 2 inches Tuesday night into Wednesday morning\nwith heavy rain possible, but there is still quite a bit of\nuncertainty for coverage and currently, areas south of I-80 are \nmore favored for higher coverage. The SPC Day2 outlook also has\na level 1 for severe thunderstorms into the far western part of\nthe area. This potential may also be dependent on the location \nof the precipitation and if convection ends up further south.\n\nPrecipitation may end for most of the area Wednesday morning\nwith the daylight hours on Wednesday mainly dry, especially\nnorth of I-80. Showers and scattered thunderstorms are then\nexpected to develop and spread back across the area late\nWednesday night into Thursday ahead of strong cold front that\nwill move across the area Thursday night. Precipitable water\nvalues area also in the 1.5 to 2 inch range on Thursday with\nheavy rain again possible.\n\nWith strong cold air advection expected Thursday night into\nFriday, northwest winds may gust into the 35-40 mph range late\nThursday night into Friday. There still remains uncertainty for\nhow cold the airmass will be behind the front but considering\nhow warm temps will be through midweek, it will be much colder\nwith Saturday morning perhaps the coldest with lows in the\nlower/mid 30s. The ECMWF had been warmer and the GFS seems to be\ncoming around to that solution as high pressure passes the area\nSunday morning and then winds turn southerly with moderating/\nwarmer temps by Sunday. cms",
